// @C117 — a linked group's members share one element layout.
//
// loft#1385 — a linked group whose members disagree about NULLABILITY is refused.
//
// Two rules meet here and cannot both hold.  `(Col-Group)` in `formal/collections.md` says
// membership is "not about whether the element is dense (`vector<E>`) or nullable
// (`vector<E?>`)", so all three members below are ROUTES to one record set.  `(N-Dense)` in
// `formal/types.md` says a `vector<E>` stores `E` and its elements are non-null unless the
// author wrote `vector<E?>`.  One record set that may hold absence cannot be read through a
// non-null element type — and the records are not even the same shape, since a nullable
// element is the tagged `__nullable<E>` (a discriminant plus the payload) and a dense one is
// `E` itself.
//
// Both silent alternatives were measured before the refusal was chosen.  Left as it was, the
// dense member falls out of its own group: a write through it reaches nothing else, and `len`
// of the member that never received the record is a legal `0`.  Made to join — by comparing
// the element through the nullable peel, which is the obvious fix — it RECEIVES the record and
// misreads it: `a[0].n` answered `7` and `a[0].k` answered `2`, the `Some` discriminant.  That
// is loft#1134's misread, a zero turned into garbage, and it is worse.
//
// So the declaration has no coherent meaning and is declined where the group would form, with
// a message naming the cure.  That is `D-bind-17`'s direction for `&τ?` and what the freeze
// axis wants: a refusal is loud, and both alternatives here are silent.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: holds `E` while another member of its record set holds
// @falsified-at: 19aecade — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1, native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/1 -> 0/1 (the control COMPILES this and answers 1/0/0)
// The group-formation walk runs once per field as the struct is built, so the refusal is
// checked in every declaration order — measured on all four of `{a, b, h}`, `{h, a, b}`,
// `{a, h, b}` and `{b, a, h}`, each refused.  Only one can be spelled in a file the parser
// stops at, so this is the keyed-LAST one and the others are the record.
struct E { k: integer, n: integer }
struct W { a: vector<E>, b: vector<E?>, h: hash<E[k]> }

fn main() {
  w = W { a: [], b: [], h: [] };
  w.a += [E { k: 1, n: 1 }];
  println("{len(w.a)}");
}
